        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(gateway): preserve local access for specific binds",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`gateway.bind: \"custom\"` could expose the Gateway only on the configured interface while local clients still connected to `127.0.0.1`. The dashboard then either failed or selected a non-loopback URL carrying the control token. `gateway --force` could also miss an interface-specific listener, and invoking `dashboard` from a dirty watched checkout could rebuild the shared `dist` tree under the running Gateway.\n\nCloses #98476.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Claim `127.0.0.1` as a required same-process alias before a specific IPv4 listener; fail startup on alias or primary-listener collisions.\n- Preserve the existing loopback fallback for tailnet binds, but reject tailnet-to-wildcard fallback.\n- Deliver dashboard credentials only after the configured endpoint is reachable and listener inspection proves the selected IPv4 address plus loopback belong to the same PID.\n- Inspect concrete listeners before generic bind probes in `gateway --force`, so interface-specific stale processes are found.\n- Treat `dashboard` as a stamped Gateway client in `run-node`, including a post-lock recheck, so it does not destructively rebuild a dirty watched checkout.\n- Document that a supervised installed Gateway must be stopped before a watcher owns the same port.\n\nThe change removes one fallback surface (tailnet to wildcard) and adds no config or default surface. Existing `gateway.bind` values and the loopback fallback remain supported.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCustom IPv4 binds keep local dashboard and approval clients on loopback while preserving the configured network listener. Credential-bearing dashboard URLs fail closed when listener ownership cannot be proven. Watch mode remains stable when dashboard commands run, and `--force` now finds interface-specific listeners.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox: 786 focused tests passed across Gateway, dashboard, force-port, watcher, and launcher shards on the current PR head.\n- Blacksmith Testbox: exact changed-surface gate and build run on the PR head.\n- Live macOS watch: `TERM=dumb pnpm gateway:watch` remained alive with exactly `127.0.0.1:18789` and the configured private IPv4 listener owned by one PID.\n- Two consecutive `pnpm openclaw dashboard --no-open --yes` runs exited 0, emitted no build output, returned only the loopback URL, kept the token out of terminal output, and preserved the same listener topology for 34 seconds.\n- Fresh Codex autoreview: no accepted/actionable findings.\n",
